syntax: zsh allows arrays and elements in assignments before a command

zsh applies an array assignment, or one to an array element, before a
command for the duration of that command, as it does for scalars, so
zsh -n accepts, and zsh runs,

	f() { print $#a $a[2]; }
	a=(1 2 3) f            # 3 2
	ZSHZ_KEEP_DIRS=( $dir ) zshz --add $dir
	in_alias=() highlight $start $end

The parser rejected all of these with "inline variables cannot be arrays",
which is right for bash but not for zsh.

With LangZsh, the assignments of a call may now have an Array or an Index.
Bash still rejects them as before.

Reproducer, before this change: `a=(x y) foo` with LangZsh failed with
"inline variables cannot be arrays".

